24:B. G., Sevoyan G. G., Yesayan A. H. Impact of ceramic packaging on the quality and safety of Armenian fermented dairy
28:    Background: Packaging is essential for food safety, as it acts as a barrier against contaminants that can cause spoilage
29:    and affect human health. The Armenian fermented dairy product, matsoun, is packaged not only in plastic and glass but
30:    also in clay pots. However, the impact of traditional packaging on the quality and safety of matsoun, especially heavy
31:    metal migration, remains under-researched.
33:    Objective: This study aimed to investigate the impact of ceramic packaging material on the quality and safety of
34:    matsoun during storage. Specifically, it compared the physicochemical properties (pH, titratable acidity), lactic acid
35:    bacteria (LAB) populations, and metal migration during storage.
37:    Methods: Six samples of matsoun were prepared and packaged in sterilized glass and ceramic containers. The samples
38:    were stored at 4 °C and analyzed on days 1, 7, and 15 of storage. The parameters measured included pH, titratable
39:    acidity, and LAB counts (coccus and bacillus). Heavy metal migration (Cd, Pb, Hg, As) was analyzed using atomic
42:    Results: The pH of samples packaged in ceramic and glass containers decreased over 15 days of storage, from 4.61 to
43:    4.24 and 4.6 to 4.27, respectively. Titratable acidity increased in both groups, reaching 1.2 % lactic acid. A decrease in
46: bacilli was observed in ceramic-packaged matsoun. The lead concentration remained constant at 0.0045 ppm across all
47: samples. In ceramic containers, cadmium concentration increased from 0.0015 ppm to 0.0029 ppm by day 15.
49: Conclusion: This study observed a decrease in pH, an increase in titratable acidity, and a relatively constant number of
50: lactic acid bacteria indicating ongoing fermentation. The results showed nondetectable concentrations of arsenic (As)
51: and mercury (Hg). The lead (Pb) concentration in all samples remained within the World Health Organization’s (WHO)
52: allowed limit of 0.02 ppm. However, the concentration of cadmium (Cd) concentration in ceramic-packed increased,
53: exceeding the WHO limit of 0.002 ppm. Further research is necessary to identify the sources of cadmium
