10:Quantification of aluminum and heavy metal contents
11:in cooked rice samples from Thailand markets using
12:inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry
13:(ICP‑MS) and potential health risk assessment
16:    Department of Applied Radiation and Isotopes, Faculty of Science, Kasetsart University, Bangkok, 10900, Thailand
21:This work quantified contents of Al and heavy metals, namely Cr, Fe, Cu, Zn, As, Cd, and Pb, in cooked rice samples prepared using
22:five different cooking utensils (new and used Al cookers, a Teflon-coated Al cooker, a stainless steel cooker, and a glass beaker)
23:and four different water conditions (tap water, de-ionized water, acidic water, and basic water) in order to assess metal leaching
24:from cooking utensils and potential health risks to local consumers. The result showed that Al contents (76.50 mg kg-1 in raw rice
25:and 76.83 mg kg-1 in cooked rice samples) and Zn contents (22.86 mg kg-1 in raw rice and 22.43 mg kg-1 in cooked rice samples)
26:represented the two largest contents of tested elements, in which contents of Al > Zn > Fe > Pb ≈ Cu > Cr >As ≈ Cd. However,
27:no strong indication of substantial metal leaching from cooking utensils to cooked rice were observed in this work as the metal contents
28:in cooked rice samples did not significantly differ from raw rice grains (p < 0.05). The result also indicated that the estimated weekly
29:intake of Al and Pb associated with rice consumption of a person who consumed approximately 0.3 kg of rice per day (181.07 mg
30:kg-1 week-1 and 4.85 mg kg-1 week-1, respectively) was greater than the provisional tolerance weekly intake (PTWI) recommended by
31:the joint FAO/WHO committee for a person who weighs 60 kg (120 mg kg-1 week-1 and 1.5 mg kg-1 week-1, respectively). In terms
32:of the assessment of non-carcinogenic and carcinogenic health risks, the hazard index (HI) calculated from all concerned metals
33:was 9.18, with the largest contributions from Pb (3.19), As (2.67), and Al (1.51), indicating a potential non-carcinogenic risk, while
34:the total cancer risk (CRt) was 2.45×10-2, with the largest contributions from Pb (1.0×10-2) and Cd (1.2×10-2), indicating serious
35:carcinogenic risks to local consumers.
